@page "/components/ai/rendererboundary"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/rendererboundary"
            Title="AiRendererBoundary"
            Description="BitAiRendererBoundary contains a failure inside one custom renderer of AI output, so the rest of the conversation survives it and the reader sees which piece failed." />

<DemoPage Name="AiRendererBoundary"
          Description="BitAiRendererBoundary contains a failure inside one custom renderer, so the rest of the conversation survives it. Custom renderers are handed data a model produced - shapes their author did not anticipate, and values that are still streaming - and without a boundary one such shape throws during rendering and takes the whole page with it. With one, the failed piece is replaced by a notice that says which piece failed, and the next render tries it again. It adds no element around content that renders."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          GitHubAIUrl="RendererBoundary/BitAiRendererBoundary.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/RendererBoundary/BitAiRendererBoundaryDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            Every renderer registered with <b>BitAiRendererRegistry</b> - for a part kind
            (<BitLink Href="/components/ai/messagepartview">BitAiMessagePartView</BitLink>), a tool
            (<BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolcallview">BitAiToolCallView</BitLink>), a structured schema
            (<BitLink Href="/components/ai/structureddataview">BitAiStructuredDataView</BitLink>), an artifact kind
            (<BitLink Href="/components/ai/artifactview">BitAiArtifactView</BitLink>), a code language, the math or the citations of the
            Markdown (<BitLink Href="/components/ai/markdownview">BitAiMarkdownView</BitLink>) - is already rendered inside one, labelled
            with the <b>RendererFailed</b> text of the strings and the name it was registered for; so is every component a model generates
            (<BitLink Href="/components/ai/generativeuiview">BitAiGenerativeUiView</BitLink>, labelled with <b>ComponentFailed</b>). Wrap
            your own templates of model output in one the same way. It is Blazor's <b>ErrorBoundary</b>: it catches what its
            content throws while rendering, in its lifecycle methods and in its event handlers - never what a fire-and-forget task throws -
            and Blazor logs each failure it catches. The message of the exception is the tooltip of the notice, so keep secrets out of the
            exceptions a renderer throws. See the <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/custom-tool">Custom tool UI</BitLink> and
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/structured">Structured output</BitLink> scenarios.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example1CodeFiles" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Wrap the renderer in it. Content that renders is rendered as it is, with no element of the boundary's own around it. Content
                that throws - here a card written for a <b>degrees</b> field, handed a forecast without one - is replaced by a notice; point at
                it for the message of the exception. Whatever is outside the boundary goes on as before.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiRendererBoundary>
                    <WeatherCard Json="@osloForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                <BitAiRendererBoundary>
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Label" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                <b>Label</b> is what the notice says once the content has failed - the <b>RenderFailed</b> text of the <b>BitAiStrings</b> in force
                when it is not set. Name the piece that failed: the built-in views say <b>The renderer for '...' failed</b>, with the tool, part kind
                or schema it was registered for.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiRendererBoundary Label="The weather card could not show this forecast">
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Recovery" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                Every render of the boundary by its parent tries the content again, which is how a renderer that fails on a partial value recovers
                once the value is complete. The forecast below streams in a few characters at a time: the card fails on every part of it that is
                not yet whole JSON, and renders once it is.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="StreamForecastAsync" Disabled="isStreaming">Stream the forecast</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <code>@(streamed.Length > 0 ? streamed : "nothing received yet")</code>

                @if (streamed.Length > 0)
                {
                    <BitAiRendererBoundary Label="Waiting for the complete forecast">
                        <WeatherCard Json="@streamed" />
                    </BitAiRendererBoundary>
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiRendererBoundaryParams</b> sets the look of every boundary inside it - the boundaries the
                built-in views render around the renderers registered with them included, so a <b>BitParams</b> around a chat restyles the
                notice of every renderer that fails in it. The content and the <b>Label</b> are not among them: they are what makes one
                boundary the one it is. It is a default, not an override: a boundary keeps what it sets itself (the last one sets
                <b>Styles</b> of its own, which replace the cascaded ones).
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitParams Parameters="rendererBoundaryParams">
                    <BitAiRendererBoundary>
                        <WeatherCard Json="@osloForecast" />
                    </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                    <BitAiRendererBoundary>
                        <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                    </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                    <BitAiRendererBoundary Styles="@(new() { Notice = new() { Root = "border-style: dotted;" } })">
                        <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                    </BitAiRendererBoundary>
                </BitParams>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                The boundary has no element of its own while its content renders, so <b>Style</b>, <b>Class</b>, <b>Styles.Root</b> and
                <b>Classes.Root</b> land on the notice that stands in for failed content - a block holding a <b>BitTag</b>, which
                <b>Styles.Notice</b> and <b>Classes.Notice</b> (<b>BitTagClassStyles</b>) and the <b>--bit-Tag-*</b> variables restyle. The tag is
                an outlined one in the error color, which its own color variables do not change.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiRendererBoundary Style="padding: 0.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem; background-color: #fef2f2">
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                <BitAiRendererBoundary Class="custom-boundary">
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                <BitAiRendererBoundary Styles="@(new() { Notice = new() { Root = "border-style: dashed", Text = "font-style: italic" } })">
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                <BitAiRendererBoundary Classes="@(new() { Root = "custom-boundary-root", Notice = new() { Icon = "custom-boundary-icon" } })">
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>

                <div style="--bit-Tag-radius: 0; --bit-Tag-font-size: 1rem; --bit-Tag-min-height: 2.5rem;">
                    <BitAiRendererBoundary>
                        <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                    </BitAiRendererBoundary>
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the notice reads from the right, its icon at the right of its label. <b>Dir</b> sets the direction
                of the notice alone; the content the boundary protects takes the direction of the page around it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiRendererBoundary Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Label="کارت آب و هوا نتوانست این پیش‌بینی را نشان دهد">
                    <WeatherCard Json="@unexpectedForecast" />
                </BitAiRendererBoundary>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
